---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "ship-motion-laws-2026-11-04",
);
const corvette = {
  version: 2,
  chassis: "corvette",
  side: "ion",
  stabilizers: true,
  hull: [null, null, null],
  aft: [null, null, null],
};

// Historical v1 input retained for provenance. The current Motion instrument
// rejects per-vehicle `changes`; this object is displayed as data and never run.
const historicalB1Configuration = {
  version: 1,
  burn: "course",
  vehicles: [
    {
      label: "High cruise 340, current force",
      loadout: corvette,
      changes: { ion: { maxSpeed: 340, acceleration: 220, bonus: 0 } },
    },
    {
      label: "Cruise 234.5, current force",
      loadout: corvette,
      changes: { ion: { maxSpeed: 234.5, acceleration: 220, bonus: 0 } },
    },
    {
      label: "Cruise 234.5, reduced force",
      loadout: corvette,
      changes: { ion: { maxSpeed: 234.5, acceleration: 110, bonus: 0 } },
    },
  ],
} as const;
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Build choices must become flight character</h2>
    <p>
      A designer adds equipment to a ship, doubles its engines, or angles a pair
      of thrusters. Each choice should create an understandable change in how
      the ship gains speed, turns, carries momentum and recovers. Those changes
      must leave room for different useful ships: a powerful launch need not
      mean the highest cruising speed, and a heavy ship need not be merely a
      worse light one. The current mechanics work, but the owner finds their
      range and depth insufficient for developing the game's ship design.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tension is between expressive construction and readable control. If
      every engine arrangement needs its own exception, designers cannot reason
      about new builds. If one physically elaborate rule makes normal turns and
      recovery laborious, the game loses its arcade character. Void Drift needs
      a small, coherent set of relationships that can produce varied ships while
      keeping two-control flight understandable.
    </p>
    <p>
      This cycle asks which shared rule should translate an assembled ship and
      pilot input into linear and rotational motion. Begin with controlled
      flight without gravity, then require that the same rule remains coherent
      through the existing gravity laws. The outcome is a flight-law decision
      with demonstrated tradeoffs, not final fleet balance. A rule must explain
      both what extra machinery buys and what the pilot gives up.
    </p>
    <aside>
      <strong>Production update:</strong>
      the accepted assembled-ship cruise model is now implemented. See the
      <a href="/lab/ship-propulsion">current propulsion reference</a>
      and
      <a href="/lab/instrument">live Motion instrument</a>. This study's earlier
      baseline text and saved captures describe the previous production law;
      they have not been rerun and are not evidence for the current model.
    </aside>

    <h3>01.1 / The first decision and its boundaries</h3>
    <p>
      Settle the minimum common law for mass, thrust composition, speed and
      turning. “Common” means consistent relationships and explicit component
      properties; it does not require one equation, identical engine response,
      or the removal of every intentional hull distinction. Existing ships are
      reference cases, not numerical targets that all alternatives must
      reproduce.
    </p>
    <p>
      The working design hypothesis is that these relationships can explain
      useful differences between builds without accumulating hull-specific
      corrections. It fails if the rule works only for a symmetric example,
      needs hidden compensation to make ordinary layouts fly, or offers variety
      that pilots cannot understand or recover from. No motion law has yet been
      selected.
    </p>

    <h3>01.2 / The questions the law must answer</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Mass and inertia.</strong>
      What should added equipment cost in acceleration, braking, turn-in and
      stopping a turn? Does mass also change attainable engine-driven speed?
      Does placement matter separately from total mass? A moving centre of mass
      and component-derived rotational inertia are options to justify, not
      prerequisites. Engine additions must include their added mass when judging
      their benefit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One engine and many.</strong>
      What determines an engine's force at rest, while already moving, and when
      opposing motion? How do aligned, angled, opposed and mixed-family engines
      combine? Separate useful acceleration from eventual speed, and separate
      commanded thrust from thrust actually delivered. Pulse and charge
      behaviour may remain distinct without giving each family an incompatible
      composition rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Speed and momentum.</strong>
      Distinguish speed attainable under sustained propulsion from instantaneous
      speed, directional thrust effectiveness and any absolute simulation limit.
      State the reference frame. What happens after release, after turning
      across existing momentum, and above the engine-driven range? Whether extra
      engines raise that range, and whether gains diminish, must be decided
      before selecting a curve.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Turning and stabilization.</strong>
      Explain how engine angle and lever arm affect translation and rotation,
      individually and together. What do left, right, both and neither command?
      What can stabilizers actually counteract, how quickly, and with what
      translation or thrust cost? Include insufficient or absent stabilization
      where the build permits it. Whether forward speed changes turning
      authority is an explicit choice. Do not silently add another player
      control or unlimited corrective torque.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>External acceleration.</strong>
      A well must not be mistaken for another engine. Explain what propulsion
      and stabilization can do while gravity changes the ship's velocity, and
      what momentum remains on exit. Decide whether excess speed persists or
      decays and why that behaviour serves play. A high peak near a body alone
      is not evidence of a useful exit or a controllable manoeuvre.
    </p>

    <h3>01.3 / Evidence that would make the decision credible</h3>
    <p>
      Start with a small contrasting set of real builds: a light craft, a
      heavier craft and an angled or asymmetric propulsion case. Compare one
      meaningful change at a time: extra non-propulsive mass; extra identical
      engines with their mass; a mixed engine set; a changed angle or lever; and
      changed stabilization. Use controlled fixtures only where production
      loadouts cannot express a necessary case, and label them as such.
    </p>
    <p>
      For each candidate, explain the predicted tradeoff before looking at
      paths. Observe acceleration, speed over time, turn rate, turn stopping
      time, heading versus travel direction and recovery distance. Use world
      units and seconds, so camera zoom cannot masquerade as a handling change.
      Time to a stated fraction of cruise speed is useful only when that
      candidate has a defined cruise speed; otherwise compare a common speed or
      fixed-duration burn. Numerical thresholds remain to be established through
      exploration rather than inherited from the supplied proposal.
    </p>
    <p>
      Repeat matched flights with no well and with one configured well, holding
      build, initial state and input sequence fixed. Distinguish coasting from
      powered passes and contacts from successful flybys. Record the law, well
      settings, power mode and elapsed time. Include release, reversal,
      both-input flight, weak stabilization and repeated turning at high speed:
      a claimed speed bound must survive changes in heading and engine mix.
    </p>
    <p>
      A satisfying result explains the direction of each change, supports at
      least two useful handling characters, and keeps its stated guarantees when
      forces compose. Replays establish reproducibility and numerical behaviour;
      hands-on flight establishes whether control is readable and recovery
      satisfying. The designer judges those qualities. Curves alone cannot
      establish fun, combat balance or a winner.
    </p>

    <h3>01.4 / Existing means and the missing experiment</h3>
    <p>
      The current build-flight calculation already uses total fitted mass,
      angled thrust and torque, persistent angular velocity and braking
      stabilizers. Ion thrust aggregates with diminishing speed gains; other
      families retain their own speed limits and delivery timing. Rotational
      inertia uses a chassis factor times mass, and some hulls have explicit
      drive rules. This provides a working baseline, not evidence that its
      particular limits are the desired future law.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/lab/instrument">Ship tuning</a>
      compares editable builds under fixed burns with supplied power, without
      gravity. <a href="/lab/gravity-tuning">Gravity tuning</a> already adds one
      mass-based well and a no-well reference through the shared calculation,
      but keeps its ships fixed. It supports inverse and inverse-square falloff,
      a pull cut-off and body contact. Use these current laws; choosing new
      gravity constants is a separate decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      The missing capability is comparing candidate ship laws and controlled
      build changes in that same well experiment. Mass, geometry and candidate
      law selection are not ordinary instrument settings today. Keep proposed
      extensions in this study; update the instrument specification when they
      are approved. Reuse the shared production simulation for comparisons. The
      initial mathematical comparison can proceed without this extension, but
      gravity compatibility cannot be declared demonstrated without it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Builder test flight currently removes planets, retains a small sun and
      still accounts for power. A later hands-on trial needs a repeatable planet
      setup and an explicit unlimited-energy option—the game's equivalent of
      “unlimited fuel”—to isolate handling from endurance. That option should
      supply power without erasing engine response timing or changing mass.
      Normal-power flight is a subsequent integration check. These are evidence
      needs, not tool features implemented or approved by this problem
      statement.
    </p>

    <h3>01.5 / The dependency order</h3>
    <p>
      First resolve propulsion and rotation in empty space with supplied power:
      what a build does, why it does it, and what the two controls request.
      Treat translation and rotation together where angled engines couple them;
      a straight-line speed curve alone cannot settle this first problem. Then
      test the surviving rule through a fixed gravity well and hands-on flight.
      If it fails there, reopen the specific motion assumption rather than
      compensate by retuning the well.
    </p>
    <p>
      Engine-family identities and fleet tuning follow the common law. Energy
      costs and endurance follow demonstrated handling. Builder performance
      indicators follow quantities the law can truthfully predict. Combat
      balance, component destruction, freely placed components and optional
      flight-computer upgrades are adjacent design problems, not requirements
      for this cycle. Their relevance can be recorded without implementing them.
    </p>

    <h3>01.6 / How to use the supplied proposal</h3>
    <p>
      The owner's deliberately naive-agent PDF contributes possibilities:
      mass-dependent speed, diminishing engine returns, directional force taper,
      coupled rotation, stabilization and gravity overspeed. Its Double-Diamond
      instructions do not govern this study. Its equations, physical analogies,
      claims about other games, engine archetypes and numerical targets are
      unvalidated hypotheses, not adopted project facts. In particular, an
      “exhaust ceiling,” “structural impedance” or an overspeed wake must earn
      its place as a game rule; the name is no evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Exploration should compare coherent alternatives against the current
      baseline, not merely tune the PDF's preferred formula. It must state which
      parts of a candidate are physical modelling, deliberate arcade rules or
      control assistance. No particular logarithm, drag law, centre of mass
      model or stabilization policy is selected here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Implementation references:
      <code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/build-flight.ts</code>,
      <code>schemas/loadout.ts</code>
      and <code>schemas/ion.ts</code> in that library;
      <code>packages/mode-a/src/lib/simulation.ts</code>;
      <code>packages/core/src/lib/physics/Physics.ts</code>. Owning contracts:
      <code>docs/specs/motion-instrument.md</code>,
      <code>docs/specs/gravity-tuning.md</code>
      and
      <code>docs/specs/gravity-field.md</code>. This page records the problem;
      production behaviour and standing design principles are unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Exploration</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>02.1 / Hull identity with engine-driven speed upgrades</summary>
      <details>
        <summary id="exploration-02-1">
          02.1 / Hull identity with engine-driven speed upgrades
        </summary>
        <h4>What the comparison established</h4>
        <p>
          Acceleration and cruising speed can express different ship characters.
          In the all-ion, supplied-power comparison, a five-ion Corvette
          initially accelerated harder than a one-ion Wasp: 606 versus 361 u/s².
          With illustrative cruising speeds of 360 and 480 u/s respectively,
          Wasp overtook the Corvette in speed after about 1.6 seconds. More
          engines improved acceleration after accounting for their mass; extra
          equipment mass lengthened the climb to speed.
        </p>
        <p>
          These results used the shared flight calculation from rest in empty
          space, with both controls held for the first six seconds of B2. A
          per-build ion limit and zero ion speed bonus represented the trial
          cruise values. The comparison supports separating acceleration from
          cruising speed. Its values are experimental examples; the additive
          model below remains to be demonstrated. Turning and gravity behaviour
          remain open.
        </p>
        <h4>Decided relationship</h4>
        <p>
          <strong
            >Build cruising speed = hull base speed + the sum of fitted engines’
            speed contributions.</strong
          >
          Engines therefore improve both acceleration and attainable cruising
          speed. Hull base speed and each engine's contribution are independent
          game-design variables, available for later tuning of individual ships
          and families.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hull's permitted mounts and compatible engines bound the available
          speed increase. This model uses that implicit maximum rather than a
          separate hull ceiling. A large difference between hull base speeds can
          preserve Wasp's speed advantage even when a heavy cruiser mounts many
          more engines. The size of that difference, each engine's contribution
          and the final fleet values belong to subsequent balance work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Initial acceleration continues to follow delivered forward force
          divided by fitted mass. The trial's linear taper provides a starting
          candidate for approaching the build's calculated cruising speed. That
          speed is a propulsion property; gravity can supply additional
          velocity. The full response to overspeed, angled thrust and mixed
          engines is still to be explored. Flight feel requires hands-on review.
        </p>
        <h4>Next investigation</h4>
        <p>
          Demonstrate the additive speed relationship alongside mass-dependent
          acceleration. Compare Wasp with basic and upgraded Corvette builds,
          using illustrative values to expose the relationship. Establish how
          the calculated build speed feeds the flight rule, and identify which
          choices remain for engine composition and later tuning.
        </p>
      </details>
    </details>
    <h3 id="exploration-02-2">
      02.2 / Additive build speed works in the tested builds
    </h3>
    <p>
      Hull base speed plus fitted engine contributions produced the intended
      cruising-speed differences and upgrades. With illustrative hull bases of
      360 u/s for Wasp and 180 u/s for Corvette, and +35 u/s per ion, the
      one-ion Wasp reached towards 395 u/s, the two-ion Corvette towards 250
      u/s, and the five-ion Corvette towards 355 u/s.
    </p>
    <p>
      Including engine mass, initial acceleration was respectively 361, 246 and
      606 u/s². The upgraded Corvette accelerated hardest and gained cruising
      speed over its basic build, while Wasp retained the highest eventual
      speed. Analytical predictions and simulated readings agreed closely at one
      and six seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      The comparison used the shared production calculation from rest in empty
      space, with supplied power and both controls held during B2's first six
      seconds. Each calculated build cruise was emulated through the per-vehicle
      ion speed limit with zero bonus. For these all-ion straight burns,
      acceleration followed force divided by fitted mass, multiplied by
      remaining speed headroom. The analytical relationship was v(t) = V × (1 −
      exp(−a₀t/V)), for constant forward thrust from rest below cruising speed
      V.
    </p>
    <h4>Conclusion</h4>
    <p>
      <strong
        >The additive cruising-speed model works for the tested builds.</strong
      >
      Hull and engine speed properties control the destination; force and fitted
      mass control the climb. This relationship is retained for the next
      exploration. The current ion acceleration produces a faster build-up than
      intended. Reducing ion force lengthens the climb while preserving
      calculated cruising speeds; its numerical tuning remains later work. Mixed
      engines, directional propulsion and gravity remain subsequent
      investigations.
    </p>

    <h3 id="exploration-02-3">
      02.3 / Angled thrust and recovery create distinct paths
    </h3>
    <p>
      The existing flight calculation produces distinct bends, hooks and
      recovery curves with lower cruising-speed settings. Angled main engines
      change translation and rotation together. Small stabilizers also deliver
      force while braking a turn after input is released, so they participate in
      the path rather than merely stopping the hull's rotation. The path
      character is a promising visual reference to retain.
    </p>
    <p>
      The comparison used Corvette with two side ions and Scout with two side
      ions plus one aft ion, each with stabilizers fitted and off. All started
      at rest at the origin, heading +x, with supplied power and no gravity, on
      B1 for 14 seconds at 60 Hz with four simulation substeps per tick. Ion
      speed settings were 234.5 u/s for Corvette and 261 u/s for Scout, with
      zero speed bonus and otherwise production settings. The capture uses Fit
      all at 1440 × 1000, with the time marker at 7 s.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/ship-motion-laws-2026-11-04/02-3-paths.png"
        alt="Recorded Corvette and Scout paths with and without stabilizers, with speed, acceleration and turn-acceleration traces."
        width="1440"
        height="1000"
        loading="lazy"
        style="width: 100%; height: auto;"
      >
      <figcaption>
        02.3 recorded comparison; a static reference for subsequent experiments.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h4>What remains unresolved</h4>
    <p>
      These paths use the existing scalar speed limiter with lower settings. The
      proposed direction-dependent speed envelope was not implemented; its
      validity remains open. Stabilizers change the commanded turn as well as
      recovery, so later path differences include both effects. Lower speed may
      explain the attractive motion, but this comparison did not vary speed
      independently to establish that cause.
    </p>
    <h4>Next investigation</h4>
    <p>
      Identify what produces this path character and whether it survives a
      gentler build-up of speed. Hold one ship, geometry, stabilizer fit and B1
      fixed; compare higher and lower cruising speed at current ion force, then
      lower speed with reduced ion force. Account for the current force setting
      changing main thrust, turning torque and stabilizer strength together.
      This investigates the relationship between speed and steering; hands-on
      feel and final numerical balance remain later work.
    </p>
    <h3 id="exploration-02-4">
      02.4 / Cruise speed and force change steering together
    </h3>
    <p class="note">
      These numerical findings are historical results from the prior ceiling
      law. The original input is retained below as data, not run by the current
      instrument. See the
      <a href="/lab/ship-propulsion">Ship propulsion reference</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 02.3 path was the visual reference: production impulses at a lower
      scalar speed setting created bends and recovery curves. The comparison
      held the Corvette's two side ions, stabilizers, 39° geometry, origin,
      heading, supplied power, empty space and B1 input sequence fixed. It
      varied the ion ceiling, then ion force at the lower ceiling, with zero
      speed bonus in all three builds.
    </p>
    <p>
      The high setting was 340 u/s, then the default; 234.5 u/s was the 02.3
      Corvette reference. The reduced-force trial used 110 u/s² at the 115-mass
      reference, half the then-current 220. These values exposed the
      relationship; none was proposed fleet balance.
    </p>
    <h4>Matched observations</h4>
    <p>
      B1 held both controls for 0–2 s, coasted 2–3 s, held left alone 3–4 s,
      then released both controls for recovery at 4–5 s. The recorded comparison
      read position, speed and spin at 3.5 s during one-sided thrust and 4.5 s
      during automatic recovery, with the same build and start for all three
      traces.
    </p>
    <p>
      With the then-current force, lowering cruise from 340 to 234.5 u/s changed
      speed at 3.5 s from 262 to 206 u/s while spin stays 149°/s; at 4.5 s,
      speed is 244 versus 189 u/s while spin is 154°/s in both. Forward position
      is shorter (712 versus 582 at 3.5 s; 951 versus 763 at 4.5 s) while
      lateral position is nearly the same. The same turn authority bends the
      lower-speed path more sharply relative to its forward travel: less
      velocity must be redirected. At cruise 234.5, halving force changed
      speed/spin at 3.5 s from 206/149 to 157/74; at 4.5 s from 189/154 to
      152/136. The reduced-force path was both slower and less bent. Ion force
      scaled main thrust, turning torque and stabilizer output together, so the
      path change cannot be attributed to gentler acceleration alone. Weaker
      steering and recovery can offset the benefit of lower travel speed.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Archived B1 input (previous ceiling model)</summary>
      <p>
        This is the original per-vehicle configuration shape used for the
        findings above. It is retained as historical data and is not parsed or
        passed to the current Motion instrument.
      </p>
      <pre
      ><code>{JSON.stringify(historicalB1Configuration, null, 2)}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <h4>Finding and limitation</h4>
    <p>
      The three traces make the path, speed, turning and recovery differences
      inspectable at the same times and spatial scale. The historical
      340-to-234.5 comparison isolated the scalar speed setting; the 234.5/110
      case was a combined force change. Production ion force drove side-main
      thrust, their turning torque, and stabilizer output from one setting. The
      shared calculation faithfully showed that coupled response, but could not
      independently tune these three outputs to explain their separate
      contributions.
    </p>
    <p>
      To separate them, hold main translation and cruise fixed while varying
      main-engine torque, then hold both fixed while varying stabilizer force
      and braking response. That needs independent candidate controls in the
      owning Motion instrument and flight calculation; it is a future
      experiment, not a change made here. This round retains the additive
      build-speed model. It investigates how speed and steering interact,
      leaving handling feel to hands-on review.
    </p>
    <h4>Next decision</h4>
    <p>
      Should turning authority be independent of forward engine force, so that
      lowering force can soften acceleration without weakening steering and
      recovery? The controlled follow-up should vary main torque and stabilizer
      strength independently at the same build mass and cruise setting.
    </p>
  </section>
  <details>
    <summary>Earlier instrument capability proposals</summary>
    <p>
      These notes were moved from the Motion instrument spec. They preserve the
      earlier hull-speed-rating exploration and are not approved instrument
      requirements. The current study decisions above take precedence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Problem framing: Ship motion: a law for assembled ships, part 01. This is
      an unimplemented investigation need, not a change to the current
      instrument contract or authorization to build it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The study needs matched comparisons of candidate propulsion/rotation laws
      and controlled mass or propulsion-geometry changes, first without gravity
      and then with one configured production mass well. Existing Ship tuning
      cannot express all these changes; Gravity tuning shares its calculation
      but fixes the ships. Resolve this boundary explicitly before implementing
      a combined experiment. Reuse the shared calculation and views, retain the
      current baseline, and record the candidate law, build, initial state, well
      and power conditions for replay. Any additional burn or measurement needed
      to expose a claimed behaviour belongs in the shared instrument contract,
      not a study-local motion chart. The exact extension is left to exploration
      and subsequent readiness/scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Exploration 02.1 retains the separation between acceleration and cruising
      speed, then selects an additive build speed: hull base speed plus fitted
      engines&#x27; speed contributions. The earlier fixed-hull-rating proposal
      is superseded.
    </p>
    <p>
      The next comparison needs a reproducible per-build cruising speed derived
      from those properties. All-ion straight flight can use the existing
      per-vehicle ion limit with zero bonus as an explicitly labeled emulation.
      A general model across engine families remains an investigation need. A
      sustained straight burn and analytical reference curves remain possible
      extensions when required by the next comparison; neither is an implemented
      capability or a prerequisite decided here.
    </p>
  </details>
  <section aria-labelledby="additive-instrument-proposal">
    <h3 id="additive-instrument-proposal">
      Instrument capability proposal: additive build speed
    </h3>
    <p>
      Exploration 02.2 demonstrates an additive all-ion build speed by emulating
      each vehicle&#x27;s calculated cruise through the existing ion
      <code>maxSpeed</code>
      setting and a zero combined-thrust bonus. This makes the proposed build
      value visible in the production flight calculation, but the configuration
      cannot record its sources. For a later mixed-family or angled-mount
      decision, a configured comparison may need explicit hull base speed,
      per-engine-family speed contributions, and the candidate composition rule,
      with the instrument showing the derived build speed alongside the
      simulation. This is an investigation capability, not an approved
      instrument contract change; decide whether it is needed after that
      question is framed.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
